      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(android): block self-package notification forwarding in allowlist mode",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nNotification forwarding in **blocklist** mode already adds OpenClaw's own package to the default block set to prevent gateway ↔ node forwarding loops.\n\nIn **allowlist** mode, there was no equivalent guard. If an operator added the OpenClaw app to the allowlist, `allowsPackage()` could return `true` for the app's own notifications, risking a self-forwarding loop.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nFail-closed on the app's own package regardless of filter mode via `NotificationForwardingPolicy.selfPackageName` and an early reject in `allowsPackage()`.\n\n## User Impact\n\nAllowlist-based notification forwarding can no longer accidentally forward OpenClaw's own notifications.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Unit tests**\n- `NotificationForwardingPolicyTest.allowsPackage_neverForwardsSelfPackageEvenInAllowlist`\n- `SecurePrefsNotificationForwardingTest.getNotificationForwardingPolicy_blocksSelfPackageInAllowlistMode`\n\n**Local proof (Windows, branch `d965722a22`)**\n```powershell\ncd apps/android\n.\\gradlew.bat :app:testPlayDebugUnitTest `\n  --tests ai.openclaw.app.NotificationForwardingPolicyTest `\n  --tests ai.openclaw.app.SecurePrefsNotificationForwardingTest\n```\n- Result: `BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 1m 38s`\n- JUnit: 18 tests, 0 failures\n\n**Note:** Device/emulator notification listener proof is not included; policy-level unit + Robolectric tests cover the changed guard.\n",
        "branch": "fix/android-notification-allowlist-self-block",
